																	<a href="/forum/t-13899160/a-deconstruction-of-the-foundations-modus-operandi-scp#post-4840095">Re: A Deconstruction of the Foundations Modus Operandi (SCP)</a>
									</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/harvoable" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7005588); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=7005588&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607480670" alt="Harvoable " style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=7005588)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/harvoable" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(7005588); return false;" >Harvoable </a></span> <span class="odate time_1607473051 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">09 Dec 2020 00:17</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13899160/a-deconstruction-of-the-foundations-modus-operandi-scp">A Deconstruction of the Foundations Modus Operandi (SCP)</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4840095">
									

<p>Well, I guess you could call it a zombie plague, but I suppose it's more nuanced than that. I mean, the infected aren't undead or anything; they'll die just as easy as any other human. Moreover, it'll be written in such a way to make it interesting. For instance, it is implied the phenomenon manifested due to the lynching of the poor Jewish gentlemen, who was repeatedly burned as a form of torture. The reason why the infection can only be destroyed through incineration is because those that killed Parisian Man used fire to burn him, even though he wasn't infected. This triggered some kind of anomalous event, fuelled by the victims hate. The murderers would've succumb to the very plague they accused him of causing, with the only escape being death through fire (coincidently, the same way they killed him.)</p>
<p>Moreover, not to bash 008 (it was a product of its time), but mine will go into more detail about the anomalous disease. For instance, the researchers find the bacteria lacks efflux pumps, drug-inactivating enzymes, or any other mechanism known to confer resistance to antibiotics. Despite this, antibiotics have no effect whatsoever. The minutiae may not be for everyone, but I like writing with a heavily scientific tone, sort of like an academic research paper.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Frankly, I don't think it's even necessary for you to write an SCP if this story is<br />
all you'll be focusing on - you could just write it in as a tale around 1609. But<br />
then again, that's completely up to you.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The SCP will be interesting in and of itself (ideally, anyway). The 1609 realisation is just kind of an <em>oh shit</em> ending I want to wrap it. Moreover, by writing a tale, it'd be difficult to explore the characterisation of Miller. The repeated neutralisation denials are what drive him to ask &quot;Why are the Foundation so adamant on containing anomalies we can't learn anything from, even when they're dangerous? Is it because we're afraid of unintended consequences, or do the O5s have their own agenda?&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How exactly does Miller find out about the conspiracy? Everything in the<br />
Foundation is under lock, key and blast gate when it comes to any sort of<br />
administration - let alone the O5.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So, Miller actually has Level 4 Access (justified, in my opinion, because he's working with an anomaly that could theoretically cause an XK-Class End-of-the-World Scenario.) That being said, he obviously doesn't have access to the O5 councils dirty little secrets. The way in which he finds out is as follows:</p>
<p>Miller is actually a GOC mole, trying to change the Foundation from the inside. By conferring with contacts within the GOC, he finds out that the supposed 1609 event never happened, and there's no record of the people that supposedly died ever existing. In fact, the GOC had never even heard of 1609. From that, Miller pieces the rest together. This would leave the reader wondering who to believe. Are the GOC lying to Miller, or are the Foundation lying and using fictional botched neutralisation attempts as a form of propaganda?</p>
<p>(This is subject to change, but the most believable way in which I could justify Miller working it out. Nobody wants a terrible cliche in which he just magically hacks the database)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Why kill Miller ala FBI/FSB if they can just amnesticize him or change his memories?<br />
It's simple, quiet and cheap. More so than staging a breach is, at any rate.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>They kill him because he's a mole. Obviously, they can't just make a Level 4 project lead disappear because people would ask questions. They can't be honest either, because they don't want people to know the Foundation was infiltrated. Therefore they orchestrate a controlled containment breach, and order Beta-7 to kill everyone within the quarantine zone (which, conveniently, includes Miller).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How are you planning to convey the whole story? I'm assuming you're going to<br />
write a different format after the article - no way is O5 letting a story like that be<br />
featured in one.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Everything up to the breach log can be accessed normally. The breach log ends with Miller typing something on his terminal when Beta-7 breach his office and shoot him. The final file will be behind level 5 access. So you go through the rigamarole of Level 5 Classified Warnings etcetera etcetera, there's mention of a memetic kill agent, then the file opens with &quot;Welcome, Overseer.&quot; Therefore, it's sort of implied the reader is a member of the O5 Council in this scenario. The contents of the final file are what Miller was writing on his terminal, detailing the shady stuff the O5's are doing, how 1609 doesn't actually exist etc. It's implied he was going to try to send this information to the GOC before he was killed.</p>
<p>So yeah, that will be the end. Hopefully it'll leave the reader wondering who was telling the truth, the Foundation or the GOC. I want to leave the reader questioning the Foundations obsession with containing everything, rather than outright destroying anomalies that pose a threat to humanity.</p>
</div>
